            <ArticleTitle>Overexpression of Sirtuin2 prevents high glucose-induced vascular endothelial cell injury by regulating the p53 and NF-κB signaling pathways.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10529-017-2487-y</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the potential role and underlying mechanism of Sirtuin2 (SIRT2) in regulating high glucose (HG)-induced vascular endothelial cell injury by using human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVECs).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">SIRT2 mRNA and protein expression levels were decreased in HG-treated HUVECs. SIRT2 overexpression increased viability, decreased apoptosis and reduced levels of reactive oxygen species in HG-treated HUVECs. SIRT2 overexpression decreased TNF-α expression (146.5 ± 22.8 pg TNF-α ml-1) relative to that in the empty vector group (263.5 ± 18.5 pg TNF-α ml-1) and decreased MCP-1 expression (63.8 ± 9.85 pg MCP-1 ml-1) relative to that in the empty vector group (105.8 ± 8.5 pg MCP-1 ml-1). SIRT2 overexpression decreased the acetylation of p53 by 33% and decreased the acetylation of NF-κB p65 by 58% in HG-treated HUVECs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">SIRT2 prevents HG-induced vascular endothelial cell injury through suppressing the p53 and NF-κB signaling pathways.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
